<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lokoja</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lokoja is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Benue rivers, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a>. An early British trading and missionary station, it served from 1900 as the first headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a>, before the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zungeru">Zungeru</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lokoja.jpg" alt="Lokoja" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ABORISADEADETONA · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Where_History_Meets_the_Confluence%22Breathing_in_1,500_feet_of_history,_(Lokoja,_Kogi)_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The area has been inhabited for hundreds of years by people of different ethnic groups, drawn in part by its nearness to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Benue rivers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> groups from Gbara near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bida">Bida</a> are believed to have been among the first to settle, on Mount Patti. The Kupa and Kakanda are said to have come downstream along the Niger, and Oworo people, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> subgroup, from Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> joined them later. The area became a centre of trade. Several peoples claim to have named the city: the Oworo give Ilu Oke Oja, the market town on the hill, the Hausa Loko Ja, a red corner, and the Nupe Patti Lukongi, the hill of doves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> and Zazzau emirs historically appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> people as political leaders of the town, while the Nupe held the post of chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. The rulers, called Maigari, included Hamza, Dauda, Musa and Muhammadu Maikarfi. The British explorer Dr William Balfour Baikie arrived first in 1854 and again in 1857, and the present settlement was established by him in 1857 on the site of an earlier model farm built during the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> expedition of 1841. The British installed Bukar, a convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, who was widely seen as a stooge of the British.</p><p>Lokoja was the capital of the British Northern Nigeria Protectorate and remained a convenient administrative town after the amalgamation of Northern and Southern Nigeria in 1914. Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a>, the first Governor-General, governed the new nation from Lokoja. Sites of this period include the spot where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a> flag was lowered in 1900 and the Bishop Crowther primary school, regarded as the first primary school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lokoja is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a>, about 165 km south-west of Abuja, and a trade centre for agricultural products. Its people include the Oworo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, Bassa-Nge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> and Ebira, who live in separate quarters but are closely related socially and politically. The Maigari of Lokoja still rules with twelve sub-chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and traditional religion are all practised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lokoja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its capital was first <b>Lokoja</b> and from 1902 Zungeru.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a></li></ul></section>
